#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Stream Stoxkart market quotes from its broadcast websocket into the Redis hash `stoxkart:quotes:live`, and keep streaming until stopped.

    bin/stoxkart/instruments/websocket_quotes                                 # instruments from the Redis set stoxkart:quotes:subscriptions
    bin/stoxkart/instruments/websocket_quotes --tokens NSE:760946,MCX:565899

    redis-cli SADD stoxkart:quotes:subscriptions NSE:760946 MCX:565899
    redis-cli HGET stoxkart:quotes:live NSE:KWIL
    redis-cli HGETALL stoxkart:quotes:instruments

**The feed.** Stoxkart's API documentation gives a binary quote websocket at `ws://inmob.stoxkart.com:7763`, which could not be reached on 2026-09-15. Stoxkart's own trading website streams quotes from `wss://broadcasting-v2.stoxkart.com/` on port 443 instead, and this script connects there. The feed takes no login: the connection header's token field is left blank, exactly as Stoxkart's website sends it. It is not documented for API users, so Stoxkart may change it without notice; the REST poller this script replaced is in git history as a fallback.

**The protocol** is Stoxkart's binary broadcast format, all little-endian. A request is an 83-byte header - a request code (1 byte), the message length (2 bytes), a 30-byte client name and a blank 50-byte token - followed for a subscription by the exchange segment (1 byte), -1 (4 bytes), a scrip count of 1 (1 byte), a blank 20-byte watchlist name and the token as 20 bytes, 129 bytes in all. The script sends the connection header (code 10), then for each instrument a trade subscription (code 12) and a depth subscription (code 23). Every answer is a run of packets, each starting with an 11-byte header: segment (1 byte), scrip id (4), a second id (4), packet length (1) and packet code (1).

| code | packet | fields used |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | trade | last price, last quantity, volume, average price, open interest, last trade time, last update time |
| 2 | depth | five levels of bid quantity, ask quantity, bid orders, ask orders, bid price, ask price |
| 3 | OHLC | open, high, low (its close is not used) |
| 6 | top of book | total quantity offered, total quantity bid |
| 32 | previous close | the previous session's close |

Circuit limits (33), 52-week range (36) and any other packet are skipped. The text `ping` is sent every five seconds and answered with `pong`; a socket silent for 30 seconds, or one that sends the text `reconnect`, is closed and opened again.

**Instruments** are `--tokens` when given, otherwise the members of the Redis set `stoxkart:quotes:subscriptions`. Each is `EXCHANGE:TOKEN`, the field format of `stoxkart:instruments:master`, with the exchange one of `NSE`, `BSE`, `NFO` or `MCX`, whose broadcast segments are 1, 4, 2 and 5. Other Stoxkart exchanges are refused, because no trade packet was seen for them on 2026-09-15 and their segment numbers are unconfirmed. Each instrument is named from `stoxkart:instruments:master`: a future or option by its `symbol_description` when that begins with its `symbol`, is longer, has no space and carries a digit (`NFO:NIFTY26SEPFUT`), any other contract, such as an MCX future whose description is `COPPER` or `GOLD 995`, by its symbol, expiry, strike and option type (`MCX:CRUDEOIL21SEP26FUT`), and anything else by its `symbol` (`NSE:KWIL`).

**Keys.**

- `stoxkart:quotes:live`, a hash of the latest tick per instrument as JSON, keyed by the instrument's name, or by `EXCHANGE:TOKEN` when the master does not name it. It is not cleared at startup.
- `stoxkart:quotes:instruments`, a hash of every subscribed `EXCHANGE:TOKEN` to its name, or to an empty string when unnamed. Replaced whole at startup.
- `stoxkart:quotes:stream`, a Redis Stream with every tick in arrival order as a `tick` field, which `bin/stoxkart/instruments/store_quotes_to_db` writes to `stoxkart.ticks`. Capped at about 1,000,000 entries.

**The tick.** Stoxkart sends each instrument's packets together in one frame, so one tick is written per instrument per frame, and only when it differs from the previous tick apart from `received_at`.

| field | from the packets |
| --- | --- |
| `id` | the instrument's name, or `EXCHANGE:TOKEN` |
| `broker`, `mode` | `stoxkart`, `full` |
| `instrument_token`, `exchange` | `EXCHANGE:TOKEN` as subscribed, the exchange |
| `last_price`, `last_quantity`, `average_price`, `volume`, `oi` | the trade packet |
| `buy_quantity`, `sell_quantity` | the top of book packet's total bid and total offered |
| `ohlc` | open, high and low from the OHLC packet, close from the previous close packet |
| `change` | the percentage change of the last price against the previous close |
| `last_trade_time` | the trade packet's last trade time plus 315532800, because Stoxkart counts seconds from 1980-01-01 UTC; null when 0 |
| `exchange_timestamp` | the trade packet's last update time plus 315532800 less 19800, because it counts India wall-clock seconds from 1980; null when 0 |
| `oi_day_high`, `oi_day_low` | null, because Stoxkart does not send them |
| `depth` | the depth packet's five `buy` and `sell` levels of `quantity`, `price` and `orders` |
| `received_at` | when this script decoded the frame |

Prices arrive as 32-bit floats in rupees and are rounded to four decimal places, which removes the float noise without touching a real tick.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM, and 2 when there is nothing to subscribe to or an instrument is not `EXCHANGE:TOKEN` on a supported exchange. A connection that fails is retried with a backoff from 1 to 60 seconds. Reads only - it places no orders.
"""

